WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.844 --> 00:00:07.567
Sam was excited as he approached Delancy's Chocolate Factory.

2
00:00:08.287 --> 00:00:13.189
It was his favorite confectionery, and he was looking forward to buying chocolates for Valentine's Day.

3
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:19.332
Inside, the aroma of chocolate and vanilla filled the air, and chocolate treats packed the shelves.

4
00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:28.476
Sam saw tantalizing dark chocolate bonbons, mint truffles, milk chocolate bars, chocolate-covered cherries, and hundreds of other types

5
00:00:28.516 --> 00:00:32.878
of candy, all sitting in pretty trays or wrapped in colorful, shiny paper.

6
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:34.960
At the counter, Mrs.

7
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.203
Delancy was busy filling heart-shaped boxes, while behind her, Mr.

8
00:00:39.223 --> 00:00:41.805
Delancy poured chocolate into tiny candy molds.

9
00:00:42.585 --> 00:00:47.569
As Sam looked around at all the chocolate, he wondered, "Where does it come from, how is it

10
00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:50.231
made, and why does it taste so good?"

11
00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:57.836
The Beginnings of Chocolate Believe it or not, chocolate was first consumed as a drink, not as candy.

12
00:00:58.528 --> 00:01:02.189
The story of chocolate begins three thousand years ago in Central America.

13
00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:10.572
The Mayans, who lived in present-day Mexico, had a sophisticated civilization, with huge temples, precise calendars, and a

14
00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:13.733
hieroglyphic writing system like the writing of the Ancient Egyptians.

15
00:01:14.853 --> 00:01:16.794
The Mayans also had a taste for chocolate.

16
00:01:17.514 --> 00:01:23.416
They were the first people to cultivate the cacao tree and collect its fruit, the cocoa bean, from

17
00:01:23.456 --> 00:01:24.576
which chocolate is made.

18
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.162
They crushed the cocoa beans and mixed them with water to make a refreshing drink.

19
00:01:29.903 --> 00:01:34.625
This cocoa drink was considered very special and reserved for wealthy nobles and warriors.

20
00:01:35.746 --> 00:01:42.369
The Aztecs, whose civilization came to power after the Mayan civilization crumbled, also enjoyed a chocolate drink.

21
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:49.473
They called it chocolatl, drank it hot rather than cold, and often added flavors, such as chili peppers.

22
00:01:50.213 --> 00:01:55.756
The cocoa bean was so valuable to the Aztecs that they used it as a form of currency—money

23
00:01:55.816 --> 00:01:57.157
really did grow on trees!

24
00:01:58.057 --> 00:02:03.638
How Chocolate Traveled to Europe The Aztecs were the first to introduce the special drink to Europeans.

25
00:02:04.178 --> 00:02:10.660
When Christopher Columbus first reached the Americas in 1492, he tried the cocoa drink but was not impressed.

26
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.541
He declared it uninteresting and rather bitter.

27
00:02:14.161 --> 00:02:18.863
The plants used to make sugar did not grow in America, so the chocolate that the Aztecs drank

28
00:02:18.903 --> 00:02:19.683
was unsweetened.

29
00:02:20.483 --> 00:02:27.710
A subsequent explorer, Hernando Cortes from Spain, found chocolatl delicious and decided to bring it back to Spain

30
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.690
for the king to try.

31
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.112
When it was presented to him, the chocolate drink did not impress the king.

32
00:02:33.792 --> 00:02:38.834
To make it more appetizing, a candy maker decided to add sugar, and it was an instant hit.

33
00:02:39.954 --> 00:02:42.055
Chocolate became the rage across Spain.

34
00:02:42.696 --> 00:02:46.457
The first chocolate makers were Spanish monks, who lived hidden in monasteries.

35
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:51.819
The king of Spain wanted to keep chocolate a secret from the rest of Europe, and the secret

36
00:02:51.859 --> 00:02:53.400
lasted for over one hundred years.

37
00:02:54.048 --> 00:03:01.293
When Queen Maria Theresa of Spain married King Louis XIV of France in 1660, she brought chocolate to

38
00:03:01.333 --> 00:03:05.236
France, and it wasn't long before its popularity spread across Europe.

39
00:03:05.817 --> 00:03:12.461
Soon, Europe was full of upscale "chocolate houses," where people could sit down and enjoy the novel drink—just

40
00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:14.823
as people enjoy coffee in cafés today.

41
00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:22.393
How Chocolate Reached the Masses Because both cocoa beans and sugar were very expensive, the new cocoa drink

42
00:03:22.413 --> 00:03:27.095
was available only to the elite of Europe, just as it had been in the Mayan and Aztec

43
00:03:27.135 --> 00:03:33.339
civilizations. But in the nineteenth century, a few inventions made it available to the many who couldn't afford

44
00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:33.959
it before.

45
00:03:35.079 --> 00:03:37.141
A Dutch inventor by the name of Conrad J.

46
00:03:37.161 --> 00:03:43.224
Van Houten built a special press that separated the cocoa seeds into cocoa butter and cocoa solids.

47
00:03:44.264 --> 00:03:48.967
The resulting solids could be pulverized into a fine powder, which we now call cocoa.

48
00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:55.451
Cocoa powder not only made chocolate drinks easier to make, it also made it possible to mix the

49
00:03:55.491 --> 00:04:00.153
chocolate with sugar and then recombine it with the cocoa butter to form a solid.

50
00:04:01.414 --> 00:04:08.078
Chocolate makers eagerly adopted the new process, and the first chocolate bar appeared in England in 1847.

51
00:04:08.798 --> 00:04:12.660
The Hershey Company was one of the first companies to mass-produce chocolate bars.

52
00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:19.604
It still produces some of today's most popular candy bars, including Kit Kats and Reese's Peanut Butter Cups.

53
00:04:20.464 --> 00:04:22.845
Another innovation involved the addition of milk.

54
00:04:23.525 --> 00:04:28.566
A chocolate maker by the name of Daniel Peter from Switzerland wanted to make a lighter chocolate and

55
00:04:28.706 --> 00:04:30.407
added evaporated milk to the mix.

56
00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:35.908
He first experimented with adding cheese, but apparently, that idea wasn't successful.

57
00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:40.950
Another invention that allowed the popularity of chocolate to spread was the refrigerator.

58
00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:48.232
When refrigeration became widely available in North America in the early 1900s, chocolate became easy to obtain all

59
00:04:49.932 --> 00:04:55.615
year-round. Chocolate in the Modern World Even though these inventions made chocolate more accessible and easier to make,

60
00:04:56.175 --> 00:05:00.898
the main steps of the process have not changed since the Mayans used it three millennia ago.

61
00:05:01.719 --> 00:05:04.260
Cocoa beans grow in pods on the cacao tree.

62
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.622
The pods are collected, allowed to ferment, dried, and then roasted.

63
00:05:09.383 --> 00:05:14.426
Next, the pods are cracked to separate the shells from the beans inside, which are called nibs.

64
00:05:15.326 --> 00:05:18.448
The nibs are ground into a thick paste called chocolate liquor.

65
00:05:19.228 --> 00:05:23.511
It is this chocolate liquor that is pressed to separate the cocoa powder from the cocoa butter.

66
00:05:24.072 --> 00:05:29.355
Cocoa powder and sugar are then recombined with the cocoa butter in controlled amounts to make a smooth,

67
00:05:29.656 --> 00:05:35.520
chocolatey paste that can be shaped into chocolate bars, poured into molds, or used to coat other sweets,

68
00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:36.620
such as caramels.

69
00:05:37.861 --> 00:05:42.344
Chocolate is one of the most popular types of candy, and it is enjoyed by people of all

70
00:05:42.445 --> 00:05:47.108
ages. It is often presented as a token of appreciation or a special gift.

71
00:05:47.828 --> 00:05:50.329
Chocolate is the traditional gift on Valentine's Day.

72
00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:54.652
In fact, some say that chocolate helps people fall in love.

73
00:05:55.412 --> 00:05:57.913
Chocolate is the favorite flavor for birthday cakes.

74
00:05:58.714 --> 00:06:02.596
In many different countries, kids receive chocolates to celebrate holidays.

75
00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:08.879
In the United States, every Halloween, children race from door to door to collect chocolate and other sweets

76
00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:09.880
from their neighbors.

77
00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.803
On Saint Nicholas Day, Dutch children get chocolates that are shaped like the first letter of their name.

78
00:06:16.583 --> 00:06:22.963
At Christmas time, German kids receive a calendar with a chocolate hidden under each day's date, which they

79
00:06:23.063 --> 00:06:25.324
open one at a time as Christmas Day approaches.

80
00:06:26.145 --> 00:06:30.187
On Easter, kids around the world participate in the hunt for chocolate Easter eggs.

81
00:06:30.927 --> 00:06:33.608
Chocolate is not always used as a sweet treat, however.

82
00:06:34.369 --> 00:06:40.052
In Mexico, some very popular dishes use mole, which is a chocolate sauce; it is often used on

83
00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:41.213
chicken, for example.

84
00:06:42.353 --> 00:06:47.556
Of course, the most popular way to enjoy chocolate is as a candy—the best candy of them all,

85
00:06:47.976 --> 00:06:48.597
Sam thought.

86
00:06:49.258 --> 00:06:56.425
He decided to get some heart-shaped chocolate bonbons, chocolate-covered almonds, and raspberry truffles for his special Valentine.

87
00:06:57.046 --> 00:07:00.890
But he couldn't resist and got some chocolate-covered toffee for himself too.
